WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.040
With such a huge selection on Netflix, finding
a good movie can be a daunting task.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.900
That's why we've gone through the Netflix
line-up to find the best little-seen films

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:09.400
available.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:14.120
From horror to comedy to action, here are
the best Netflix gems you probably haven't

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:14.700
seen yet.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:15.860
The Wraith

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.660
Back in the technicolor '80s, there was a
wonderful movie called The Wraith.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.820
The idea: a murdered street racer returns
from the grave to get revenge on a bunch of

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.860
street punks in an undead muscle car.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.070
As far as terrible-but-awesome premises go,
this sits somewhere near the top of the stack.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:34.580
And yet in spite of everything it's got going
against it, The Wraith actually works.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.820
It's solid B-movie material elevated to hidden
gem status by colorful characters, surprisingly

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:41.940
good special effects, and fun dialogue.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.740
The Wraith knows full well what it is, and
it doesn't take itself too seriously.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.280
Kill Me Three Times

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:51.860
Simon Pegg is a modern cult megastar, but
this hilarious crime thriller still managed

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:54.880
to fly under the radar on its 2014 release.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.900
With a kinetic style reminiscent of Guy Ritchie's
good films, Kill Me Three Times follows three

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.400
intertwined stories of ordinary people driven
to the brink, all of it linked by one man

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.360
with a very hitman mustache.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.760
A Simon Pegg hitman mustache.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:14.360
At times brutal, touching, and hilarious — sometimes
all at once — you won't find a more flat-out

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.300
fun movie than Kill Me Three Times.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:17.500
Creep

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.600
Who knew a film with a cast of exactly two
people could be so enthralling?

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.680
Yeah, you might groan at first: if Creep had
a downside, it would be the decision to make

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.140
it in jerky, handheld, found-footage style.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.200
But Creep pulls it off, and beautifully at
that.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:34.220
In fact, this may be the first film since
The Blair Witch Project that couldn't have

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.200
been filmed any other way, and arguably the
first good one.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:42.320
Creep switches seamlessly between drama, comedy,
and horror thanks mostly to a near-perfect

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.680
performance by mumbling maniac Mark Duplass.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.660
Whatever else you take away from it, there's
no doubt Creep will give you, well, the creeps.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:50.700
Bronson

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:56.310
Back in 2008, before he was a household name,
Tom Hardy starred in a bizarre biopic called

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:56.810
Bronson.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.160
In real life, Charles Bronson — whose real
name was Michael Peterson before he renamed

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:05.170
himself after the actor — has spent more
than 40 years in prison and was dubbed "the

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:08.360
most violent prisoner in Britain" — even
though he's never killed anyone.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.880
When Tom Hardy was first tapped to star in
the movie, the real Bronson was less than

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.000
impressed by the actor.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.840
But after Hardy gained a ton of weight and
studied the way the real-life criminal moved,

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:20.500
Bronson changed his tune, saying, "Tom looks
more like me than I look like me."

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:25.160
If you were impressed by Bane and Mad Max
Rockatansky, don't miss out on Bronson.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.340
It's got its weird moments, but the movie
is insanity at its best.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.180
"Ha ha."

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:35.180
Ravenous

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.420
There aren't a lot of movies that will make
you question your dinner, but from the very

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:42.800
first frame, Ravenous turns rare steak into
an entre to be regarded with suspicion.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.380
Starring Guy Pearce and Robert Carlyle, this
1999 gem plays like a horror movie on the

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.000
surface, but does so with a heart of comedic
black gold.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.400
While it begins as a seemingly straightforward
story of an army captain assigned to the remote

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:59.050
Sierra Nevada mountains after the Mexican-American
War, Ravenous quickly moves into territory

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:00.340
that's hard to classify.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:05.120
There's the horror of cannibalism in the isolated
mountains, the action of a spaghetti Western,

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:09.849
a thick smattering of tongue-in-cheek humor,
and a pulsing vein of modern satire woven

00:03:09.849 --> 00:03:10.349
throughout.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.300
All of it comes together into an extremely
entertaining, offbeat movie experience that

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.620
you won't forget anytime soon.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:20.560
Unfortunately, the film's marketing sent people
to theaters expecting a more mainstream, slapstick

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.240
comedy, not the violent, black humor they
ended up watching.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:27.240
As a result, Ravenous was chewed to pieces
by critics and limped through theaters earning

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.940
a measly $2 million—half of which came during
its opening weekend.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:32.480
Beyond the Gates

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.260
You could probably get away with calling this
movie Jumanji 2: This Time with Demons, but

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:41.280
that wouldn't be entirely fair to Beyond the
Gates, a horror thriller about two brothers

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.420
who find a VHS board game in their recently
deceased father's video store.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.960
Video board games were a real, weird thing
in the VCR's heyday, so there's probably a

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:54.000
nostalgia element to Beyond the Gates if you
were one of the 30 or 40 people who ever played

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:54.500
one.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.240
But for everyone else, the idea of a video
that knows what you're doing lends as much

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:02.440
to the fantasy element of the film as the
hellish repercussions the brothers experience

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:03.880
as they fall deeper into the game.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:06.860
Hellish, in this case, is of course totally
literal.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.100
The more the brothers play, the more the game
begins to bleed into the real world, turning

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.260
their home into the devil's suburban dreamhouse.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.280
It's a fun, twisted film, perfect for a rainy
night.

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:17.300
Look Who's Back

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:22.580
This low-key German satire is both unexpected
and surprisingly hilarious.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:26.680
Look Who's Back starts with Hitler waking
up in in a park in modern Berlin, still in

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:28.540
uniform, with no idea how he got there.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:33.260
The first people he meets think he's a crazy
hobo, the next ones think he's a Method actor

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:38.270
doing a bit, and before long, Hitler's all
over the news as the next big German comedian…and

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:39.140
the people love him.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:43.680
In fact, it's less a satire on Hitler and
more a social commentary on modern-day Germany

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:44.300
as a whole.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:46.300
On top of all that, it's pretty funny.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:50.500
Look Who's Back is definitely a weird combo,
but it's one that happens to work extremely

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:51.000
well.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:52.300
Uncanny

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.780
If you liked Ex Machina, you'll be right at
home with Uncanny, a tight, claustrophobic

00:04:56.789 --> 00:05:01.270
movie about a brilliant roboticist named David
Kressen who builds the perfect artificial

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:01.900
intelligence.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:02.700
Or is it?

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:07.140
When the robot's behavior grows more sinister,
the programmer and his newfound girlfriend,

00:05:07.159 --> 00:05:11.020
a journalist on assignment to write about
Kressen's achievements, find themselves trapped

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:15.560
in Kressen's lab with an uncannily human creature
who's suddenly become painfully unpredictable.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.260
Beautifully shot and steeped in tension you
could cut with a bonesaw, Uncanny is a fantastic

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:21.460
hidden sci-fi thriller.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:25.400
With incredible performances from everyone
involved, you won't be able to look away until

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:25.900
the end.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:27.240
The Void

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:32.379
In many ways, the John Carpenter horror thrillers
of the '80s were a genre unto themselves.

00:05:32.379 --> 00:05:36.009
Movies like The Thing and Prince of Darkness
reveled in violence and body horror without

00:05:36.009 --> 00:05:41.900
leaning on it as a pure shock tactic — and
somehow, in 2017, a small sci-fi horror film

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.160
slipped unnoticed into that exclusive genre.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:48.560
The Void is a throwback to those '80s creature
features in many ways, from the practical

00:05:48.569 --> 00:05:52.469
monster effects to the subdued, foreboding
atmosphere, but it also stands on its own

00:05:52.469 --> 00:05:54.689
as a bloody good sci-fi creepfest.

00:05:54.689 --> 00:05:55.189
Don't miss it.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:56.400
Thanks for watching!

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.500
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.760
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

